          %h3#hedbergs_theorem Hedberg’s theorem
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hedberg%27s+theorem"} Hedberg's theorem
            states that
          %p
          .num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              Suppose that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
              with
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/untruncated+decidable+equality"} untruncated decidable equality
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:A
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b:A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi A
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a =_A b
              and the type of functions from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi A
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a =_A b
              to the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+type"} empty type
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi A
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi A
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi ∅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:A, b:A \vdash \delta(a, b):(a =_A b) + (a =_A b) \to \emptyset
            %p
              Then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is a h-set.
          %p
            A proof of this theorem could be found in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hedberg%27s+theorem"} Hedberg's theorem
          %h3#RelationToInternalSets Relation to internal sets
          %p
            When using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            as the ambient
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of some
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+set"} internal set
          %p
            Note, though, that this notion of “internal set” is of a different sort from the usual notions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} internal category
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+groupoid"} internal groupoid
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+object+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} groupoid object in an (∞,1)-category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/setoid"} setoid
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presets"} presets
          %p
            The point is that to be worthy of the name “set”, a notion ought to come with “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotients"} quotients
            of
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relations"} equivalence relations
            %em not
            have quotients, such as the types in ordinary
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+dependent+type+theory"} Martin-Löf dependent type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+completion"} exact completion
            whose objects are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/setoids"} setoids
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+inductive+types"} higher inductive types
          %p
            A good way to reconcile these seemingly clashing terminologies is to talk about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+completions"} exact completions
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unary+sites"} unary sites
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-sites"} (∞,1)-sites
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topology
            allows us to “remember” to what extent our given notion has well-behaved quotients: if we have no quotients, then we use the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+topology"} trivial topology
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+topology"} regular topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+category"} exact category
            or
            = succeed "-category)," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %h3#PretoposOfHsets Pretopos of hsets
          %p
            The h-sets in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HoTT"} HoTT
            form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CE%A0W-pretopos"} ΠW-pretopos
            (
            %a{:href => "#RijkeSpitters13"}> Rijke-Spitters 13
            ). See also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
